{"kind":"assert","text":"Review for Hugh: use one release-archive check for rehearsal and publication.\n\nTailapp's v0.1.3 rehearsal passed while the publishing job wrote inside the checkout and produced dirty build metadata. The independently approved v0.1.4 repair moved publication output to runner temp; review #3111 also suggested checking cleanliness after the build. The [history and current-source evidence](release-rehearsal-review-evidence.json) separates that delivered repair from this follow-up opportunity.\n\nAt current Tailapp main, the [release workflow](https://github.com/generalbusiness-ai/tailapps/blob/b583cdc4c3bcb1f6359f3298c067ae2298b02ca7/.github/workflows/release.yml) ([file preview](tailapp-release-workflow.yml)) checks cleanliness before building. Its dry-run asset check confirms only two executable scripts and two manifest files. The [release script](https://github.com/generalbusiness-ai/tailapps/blob/b583cdc4c3bcb1f6359f3298c067ae2298b02ca7/scripts/release.sh) ([preview](tailapp-release.sh)) is already shared, and the [existing integration check](tailapp-release-test-excerpt.txt) usefully verifies the fixture-installed binary's public dependency and version. Preserve those controls.\n\nRecommendation: add one small unsigned-archive verifier and call it immediately after the existing build in both jobs, before signing in the publishing job. Give it the expected version, exact source revision and output directory. It should check the four actual archives, required files and checksums, pinned scripts, expected binary provenance and clean build metadata, plus an unchanged checkout. Reuse the existing [build conventions](tailapp-build.sh) and checks; avoid a second release-policy checklist. Read cross-platform build metadata without executing foreign binaries.\n\nAcceptance should include an actual dirty build or altered archive that the shared check rejects, and a clean complete output that passes. Keep privileged signing/publication separate; this check needs no new secret or workroom state.\n\nThe benefit is fewer release-only surprises and fewer corrective tag/review cycles. No saving is quantified. This is advice for Hugh's review, with no implementation assignment or release authorization. The current release is not alleged defective. #19084 covers installed-binary provenance; this narrower recommendation aligns the two release jobs.","body":{"stale_bases":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:4f23117c3d1e03a9a828347e926eea0ca32757d3 stale; retired bases: git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:1461e3f2c245f4ba93ab8931e626b2c78896f778, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:51be5e7d258a275fd038b31abb1d03b9190790e4","to":"hugh"}}